> I think that depends a lot on your max HP. If you're at 350 HP, that
> much AC is fine; if you're at 150, no way. There are more experienced
> people here who might tell you otherwise, though.

It may be mainly experience: i find -30 AC and 150 hp entirely
sufficient for an ascension. I've a bunch of ascension dumps saved,
and only few of them have better AC than -37; some have ACs in the -10
range upon ascension, but that's because they're wearing helms of
opposite alignment - had something like -25 while fighting through
Astral. One had a genuine ascension AC of -15 at 174 maxhp, but she
was polymorphed into a black dragon and wore an amulet of unchanging
for the second half of the game ...

I think that running into trouble with -37 AC means there's still some
way to improve your tactics (run, don't fight on the planes, control
the monster flow better on Astral ...). You'll have to add a fair bit
of AC at that point to gain a notable benefit: even slapping on a
fully-enchanted elven shield for another nine points won't make a
large difference when you're starting at -37.

And damaging spell attacks are entirely unaffected by AC - if you get
hurt by Archon's headaches and priestesses' 'open wounds', all the AC
in the world won't help you one iota.

> Conflict, in my opinion, seems to serve to decrease the damage you are
> taking per round, since fewer things are attacking you. If you're lucky,
> priests and player monsters will off one of the Riders for you, too.
> Conflict does not cause you to win Astral, but it helps. On the other
> hand, if you're carting as many pets up as you can, conflict is obviously
> going to cause problems with that strategy. In that case you are counting
> on your pets to soak hits for you instead, I think. Maybe someone else
> can describe that strategy better?

When playing with pets on the Astral Plane, i spend a lot of time in
corridors and narrow doorways - there are a few of these - and
maneuvre my pets into the way of monsters. You can shake off
pursuit/create a better tactical position by moving into the corridors
behind the high temples - great for losing the Riders, if you can get
something between you and them so their pathfinding gets confused.
With pets, magic whistles are practically mandatory, and you'll need a
way to make new friends and/or heal your existing pets (*healing and
monster charming, preferably by spell, although confused scrolls of
taming can be very nice, too).

